"GetFinalPathNameByHandleW function (fileapi.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-getfinalpathnamebyhandlew)\nciteturn29view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-getfinalpathnamebyhandlew\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 230\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # GetFinalPathNameByHandleW function (fileapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Retrieves the final path for the specified file.\nL33: \nL34: For more information about file and path names, see cite5†Naming a File .\nL35: \nL36: ## Syntax\nL37: \nL38:     `DWORD GetFinalPathNameByHandleW(\nL39:       [in]  HANDLE hFile,\nL40:       [out] LPWSTR lpszFilePath,\nL41:       [in]  DWORD  cchFilePath,\nL42:       [in]  DWORD  dwFlags\nL43:     );\nL44:     `\nL45: ## Parameters\nL46: \nL47: `[in] hFile`\nL48: \nL49: A handle to a file or directory.\nL50: \nL51: `[out] lpszFilePath`\nL52: \nL53: A pointer to a buffer that receives the path of hFile.\nL54: \nL55: `[in] cchFilePath`\nL56: \nL57: The size of lpszFilePath, in TCHARs. This value must include a NULL termination character.\nL58: \nL59: `[in] dwFlags`\nL60: \nL61: The type of result to return. This parameter can be one of the following values.\nL62: \nL63: Value  | Meaning\nL64: --- | ---\nL65: \nL66: FILE_NAME_NORMALIZED\nL67: 0x0\nL68:  | Return the normalized drive name. This is the default.\nL69: FILE_NAME_OPENED\nL70: 0x8\nL71:  | Return the opened file name (not normalized).\nL72: \nL73: \nL74: This parameter can also include one of the following values.\nL75: \nL76: Value  | Meaning\nL77: --- | ---\nL78: \nL79: VOLUME_NAME_DOS\nL80: 0x0\nL81:  | Return the path with the drive letter. This is the default.\nL82: \nL83: VOLUME_NAME_GUID\nL84: 0x1\nL85:  | Return the path with a volume GUID path instead of the drive name.\nL86: \nL87: VOLUME_NAME_NONE\nL88: 0x4\nL89:  | Return the path with no drive information.\nL90: \nL91: VOLUME_NAME_NT\nL92: 0x2\nL93:  | Return the NT device object path.\nL94: ## Return value\nL95: \nL96: If the function succeeds, the return value is the length of the string received by lpszFilePath, in TCHARs. This value does not include the size of the terminating null character.\nL97: \nL98: Windows Server 2008 and Windows Vista:  For the ANSI version of this function, GetFinalPathNameByHandleA, the return value includes the size of the terminating null character.\nL99: If the function fails because lpszFilePath is too small to hold the string plus the terminating null character, the return value is the required buffer size, in TCHARs. This value includes the size of the terminating null character.\nL100: \nL101: If the function fails for any other reason, the return value is zero. To get extended error information, call cite6†GetLastError .\nL102: \nL103: Return code  | Description\nL104: --- | ---\nL105: ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND\nL106:  | Can be returned if you are searching for a drive letter and one does not exist. For example, the handle was opened on a drive that is not currently mounted, or if you create a volume and do not assign it a drive letter. If a volume has no drive letter, you can use the volume GUID path to identify it. This return value can also be returned if you are searching for a volume GUID path on a network share. Volume GUID paths are not created for network shares.\nL107: ERROR_NOT_ENOUGH_MEMORY\nL108:  | Insufficient memory to complete the operation.\nL109: \nL110: ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER\nL111:  | Invalid flags were specified for dwFlags.\nL112: ## Remarks\nL113: \nL114: The Server Message Block (SMB) Protocol does not support queries for normalized paths. Consequently, when you call this function passing the handle of a file opened using SMB, and with the FILE_NAME_NORMALIZED flag, the function splits the path into its components and tries to query for the normalized name of each of those components in turn. If the user lacks access permission to any one of those components, then the function call fails with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED.\nL115: A final path is the path that is returned when a path is fully resolved. For example, for a symbolic link named \"C:\\tmp\\mydir\" that points to \"D:\\yourdir\", the final path would be \"D:\\yourdir\".\nL116: \nL117: When using VOLUME_NAME_DOS, the string that is returned by this function uses the \"\\\\?\\\" syntax. For more information, see cite7†CreateFile .\nL118: \nL119: When using VOLUME_NAME_GUID, the returned path will begin with a volume GUID path formatted like \"\\\\?\\Volume{xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx}\\\".\nL120: When using VOLUME_NAME_NT, the returned path is for an NT device object, and will begin with a device name such as \"\\Device\\HarddiskVolume1\\\". This type of path can not be used directly by Windows programs, as it resembles a relative path.\nL121: Some third-party drivers can create a drive letter or mount point without using the Mount Manager. If the Mount Manager was not used to create the drive, then VOLUME_NAME_DOS or VOLUME_NAME_GUID will not succeed; only VOLUME_NAME_NT will be available. To determine the drive letter for the volume device path, use the cite8†QueryDosDevice function on every drive letter until a matching device name is found.\nL122: \nL123: In Windows 8 and Windows Server 2012, this function is supported by the following technologies.\nL124: Technology  | Supported\nL125: --- | ---\nL126: Server Message Block (SMB) 3.0 protocol  | Yes\nL127: SMB 3.0 Transparent Failover (TFO)  | Yes\nL128: SMB 3.0 with Scale-out File Shares (SO)  | Yes\nL129: Cluster Shared Volume File System (CsvFS)  | Yes\nL130: Resilient File System (ReFS)  | Yes\nL131: #### Examples\nL132: The following example demonstrates the use of the GetFinalPathNameByHandle function.\nL133: \nL134:     `#include <windows.h>\nL135:     #include <tchar.h>\nL136:     #include <stdio.h>\nL137: \nL138:     #define BUFSIZE MAX_PATH\nL139: \nL140:     void __cdecl _tmain(int argc, TCHAR *argv[])\nL141:     {\nL142:         TCHAR Path[BUFSIZE];\nL143:         HANDLE hFile;\nL144:         DWORD dwRet;\nL145: \nL146:         printf(\"\\n\");\nL147:         if( argc != 2 )\nL148:         {\nL149:             printf(\"ERROR:\\tIncorrect number of arguments\\n\\n\");\nL150:             printf(\"%s <file_name>\\n\", argv[0]);\nL151:             return;\nL152:         }\nL153: \nL154:         hFile = CreateFile(argv[1],               // file to open\nL155:                            GENERIC_READ,          // open for reading\nL156:                            FILE_SHARE_READ,       // share for reading\nL157:                            NULL,                  // default security\nL158:                            OPEN_EXISTING,         // existing file only\nL159:                            FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, // normal file\nL160:                            NULL);                 // no attr. template\nL161: \nL162:         if( hFile == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)\nL163:         {\nL164:             printf(\"Could not open file (error %d\\n)\", GetLastError());\nL165:             return;\nL166:         }\nL167: \nL168:         dwRet = GetFinalPathNameByHandle( hFile, Path, BUFSIZE, VOLUME_NAME_NT );\nL169:         if(dwRet < BUFSIZE)\nL170:         {\nL171:             _tprintf(TEXT(\"\\nThe final path is: %s\\n\"), Path);\nL172:         }\nL173:         else printf(\"\\nThe required buffer size is %d.\\n\", dwRet);\nL174:         CloseHandle(hFile);\nL175:     }\nL176: \nL177:     `\nL178: Note\nL179: \nL180: The fileapi.h header defines GetFinalPathNameByHandle as an alias that automatically selects the ANSI or Unicode version of this function based on the definition of the UNICODE preprocessor constant. Mixing usage of the encoding-neutral alias with code that is not encoding-neutral can lead to mismatches that result in compilation or runtime errors. For more information, see cite9†Conventions for Function Prototypes .\nL181: ## Requirements\nL182: \nL183: Requirement  | Value\nL184: --- | ---\nL185: Minimum supported client  | Windows Vista [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL186: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2008 [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL187: Target Platform  | Windows\nL188: Header  | fileapi.h (include Windows.h)\nL189: Library  | Kernel32.lib\nL190: DLL  | Kernel32.dll\nL191: \nL192: ## See also\nL193: \nL194: cite10†File Management Functions L195: \nL196: * * *\nL197: ## Feedback\nL198: \nL199: Was this page helpful?\nL200: \nL201: Yes No No\nL202: \nL203: Need help with this topic?\nL204: \nL205: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL206: \nL207: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL208: \nL209: Suggest a fix?\nL210: \nL211: * * *\nL212: \nL213: ## Additional resources\nL214: \nL215: * * *\nL216: \nL217:   * Last updated on 2023-02-09\nL218: \nL219: ### In this article\nL220: \nL221: Was this page helpful?\nL222: \nL223: Need help with this topic?\nL224: \nL225: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify 